      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T09:10:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t understand the use case this is meant for.\n\nIs it that you want to create a tag on Gerrit via REST API but sign it locally ?",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "93cd065e_bdbabb49",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T09:13:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, exactly.\n\nThe context is, pushing tags is disabled at Google by default. So users want to be able to create signed tags using the REST API.",
      "parentUuid": "6ea52fe4_b4d8457c",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7bd00d66_22f30361",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1004034
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T09:26:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What\u0027s the point of disallowing pushing tags when Gerrit provides a REST API circumventing this policy ?",
      "parentUuid": "93cd065e_bdbabb49",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d2ce3d0d_404bbd89",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T09:31:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We only want to allow creating tags on existing commits, but not pushing tags on new commits (as this would bypass code review).\n\nWith the REST API you can only create tags on existing commits, hence it\u0027s safe to allow it.\n\nBy pushing tags you can do both, create tags an existing commits and on new commits, and hence is got disabled. It would be possible and desirable to only block pushing tags on new commits, but allow pushing tags on existing commits. This would need to be implemented by another team which doesn\u0027t have resources for this at the moment, so we are evaluating whether we can support creating signed tags via the Gerrit REST API in the meantime.",
      "parentUuid": "7bd00d66_22f30361",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "733cd53d_5ff04ad8",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1149436
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T09:40:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "On the JGit vs. git inconsistency: indeed git verifies signatures without matching principal in allowedSigners.[1] It then just verifies that the signature itself is correct and well-formed.[2] I don\u0027t quite get this; it kind of makes allowedSigners useless? JGit in its current implementation enforces it: [3].\n\nSo C git requires allowedSigners to be set, but doesn\u0027t require it to contain anything. What\u0027s the point?\n\nWe could change JGit to behave the same way. I would expect some difference in the output then so the user can tell whether or not a principal matched.\n\n[1] https://github.com/git/git/blob/e4ef0485fd78fcb05866ea78df35796b904e4a8e/gpg-interface.c#L515\n[2] https://man.openbsd.org/ssh-keygen.1#Y~3\n[3] https://github.com/eclipse-jgit/jgit/blob/ca6d673d59f27382758e1f54e7731b9d658a5730/org.eclipse.jgit.ssh.apache/src/org/eclipse/jgit/internal/signing/ssh/SshSignatureVerifier.java#L233",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b032e2a_55793cce",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1149436
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T09:47:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But git verify-tag should return a failure error code if there are no known principals?[1] So maybe JGit does it right.\n\n[1] https://github.com/git/git/blob/e4ef0485fd78fcb05866ea78df35796b904e4a8e/gpg-interface.c#L534",
      "parentUuid": "733cd53d_5ff04ad8",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5829d9d3_2fbfcebe",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T09:52:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks a lot for having a look! Much appreciated!\n\nI can\u0027t really tell if what C git does here makes sense. \n\nFor this use-case in Gerrit having JGit check that the signature itself is correct and well-formed would be nice.\n\nBefore finding out that C Git could do this, I was wondering, whether we could make the caller provide their public key and then feed this public key into JGit (via a setter on VerifySignatureCommand) so that JGit could verify the signature. Would this make sense?\n\n\u003e We could change JGit to behave the same way. I would expect some difference\n\u003e in the output then so the user can tell whether or not a principal matched.\n\nThat would be nice! Differences in the output are not a problem for us.",
      "parentUuid": "733cd53d_5ff04ad8",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "719c75c9_79d91d82",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1149436
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T10:27:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, did you check the return value of the \"git verify-tag\" command you gave? I suspect it\u0027s non-zero, indicating a failed signature verification.\n\nThe signature contains the public key used to create the signature already. See [1]. Gerrit could, for SSH signatures, extract that key and fake an allowedSigners file with the identity of the currently-logged-in user and that key. Or Gerrit hooks in its own `SigningKeyDatabase`. It could even require that the SSH key used for tagging be one of the SSH keys associated with the account.\n\nBTW, should the REST API only accept SSH signatures, or would it also accept openpgp or x509 signatures?\n\n[1] https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/blob/5e4bfe6c16924b1c21a733f3e218cfcba98e301e/PROTOCOL.sshsig#L32",
      "parentUuid": "5829d9d3_2fbfcebe",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2a6007ed_665f1ce4",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-28T11:10:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Well, did you check the return value of the \"git verify-tag\" command you gave?\n\u003e I suspect it\u0027s non-zero, indicating a failed signature verification.\n\nI didn\u0027t check this before. You are right that it\u0027s non-zero:\n\n```\n$ ssh-keygen -t rsa -q -P \"\" -f id_rsa\n$ git config gpg.format ssh\n$ git config user.signingkey id_rsa.pub  \n$ git tag -s my-signed-tag\n$ git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile\u003d/dev/null verify-tag refs/tags/my-signed-tag\nGood \"git\" signature with RSA key SHA256:OCiTv40IRSCDABWjuGVvfCwX4eqHyED8/UecPwGWKTE\nNo principal matched.\n$ echo $?\n1\n```\n\n\u003e The signature contains the public key used to create the signature already. See [1]\n\nThanks, I wasn\u0027t aware of this.\n\n\u003e Gerrit could, for SSH signatures, extract that key and fake an allowedSigners\n\u003e file with the identity of the currently-logged-in user and that key.\n\nI did consider faking an allowedSigners file, but the problem is that our servers don\u0027t have access to a regular file system. So I\u0027m not sure this is feasible for us.\n\n\u003e Or Gerrit hooks in its own SigningKeyDatabase.\n\nThis looks interesting. On first sight this looks like it could work. Thanks for suggesting this.\n\n\u003e It could even require that the SSH key used for tagging be one of the SSH keys \n\u003e associated with the account.\n\nYes, this sounds like a good idea.\n\n\u003e BTW, should the REST API only accept SSH signatures, or would it also accept\n\u003e openpgp or x509 signatures?\n\nAFAIK our use case is only for SSH signatures. Earlier I thought, if we allow one signature, we should allow all [1], but maybe it\u0027s better to restrict this to SSH signatures.\n\nAs additional context, it\u0027s the go team that wants to be able to create signed tags via REST. While our documentation says that this is not supported [2], they managed to do this using this script [3] (I just realised that this is public). So we were discussing how we can make that support more official, and having Gerrit be able to verify the signature is the missing piece here.\n\n[1] https://gerrit-review.git.corp.google.com/c/gerrit/+/494162\n\n[2] https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/rest-api-projects.html#create-tag\n\n[3] https://github.com/rsc/tmp/blob/master/signre2/main.go",
      "parentUuid": "719c75c9_79d91d82",
      "revId": "e920039b4093da17efdc10ea8c1d9b52ef24ba2d",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "365cc46e_94360baf",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
